    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          There is no single perfect humidity for a house. There is a comfortable band, roughly 30 to 50 percent, and a ceiling you should not cross, around 60. In winter the right number inside depends on the temperature outside, because the colder your windows get, the less moisture the house can hold before it starts raining on the sills.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="the-band" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: 30 to 50 Percent, Never Above 60
          </h2>

          <p>
            The figure most guidance converges on comes from the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, which advises keeping indoor relative humidity below 60 percent and, ideally, between 30 and 50 percent. That is not an arbitrary comfort range. The top of it is set by what grows in damp air, and the bottom by what happens to skin, wood and static electricity when the air is dry. Inside that window most people feel comfortable and most houses stay healthy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Relative humidity is the part that confuses people. It is not how much water is in the air; it is how much water the air holds compared with the most it could hold at that temperature. Warm air can carry far more moisture than cold air, which is why the same amount of water reads as 80 percent on a cool morning and 45 percent by mid-afternoon once the room warms up. When you set a humidifier or read a hygrometer, you are always working with relative humidity, and it always depends on temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homes the practical target splits by season. In summer, the job is usually keeping humidity under 50 to 55 percent, because warm damp air is what dust mites and mold prefer, and that is a job for air conditioning or a dehumidifier rather than a humidifier. In winter, heated air dries out and the job flips: you are trying to bring a house sitting at 15 or 20 percent back up into the 30s or low 40s without overshooting and fogging the windows.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only remember one number, make it 40 percent in winter as a starting point, then adjust down when it gets very cold outside and up slightly if the house is well sealed and the windows stay dry. If you only remember one rule, make it this: when water appears on the inside of your windows, you are above the right level for that day, whatever the dial says.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical target</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Summer, any room</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 to 50%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Above 50 to 60% favors dust mites and mold</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Winter, mild (above 20°F outside)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">35 to 45%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Windows stay warm enough to avoid condensation</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Winter, cold (0 to 20°F outside)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 to 35%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Colder glass condenses at lower humidity</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Winter, severe (below 0°F outside)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 to 30%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Accept drier air to protect windows and walls</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Absolute ceiling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The EPA's upper limit for indoor humidity</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Winter: Why the Right Setting Falls as It Gets Colder Outside
          </h2>

          <p>
            The reason a single winter setpoint fails is the window. Glass, especially older double-pane units and aluminum frames, is the coldest surface inside most houses. When warm, moist room air touches it and cools to its dew point, water condenses. The colder it is outside, the colder that glass gets, and the lower the indoor humidity at which condensation starts. A setting that is perfectly comfortable at 30°F outside can coat every window by morning at 0°F.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why furnace humidistats often carry an outdoor-temperature chart, and why many whole-house humidifier controls now measure outdoor temperature automatically and lower the target as it drops. The numbers below reflect that common guidance for a typical home with double-pane windows. Treat them as a starting point, not a law: tight new construction with good windows can sometimes run a few points higher, while drafty houses with single-pane glass or storm windows may need to run lower still.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Outdoor temperature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Suggested maximum indoor RH</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">40°F and above</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 45%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20°F to 40°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 40%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10°F to 20°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 35%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">0°F to 10°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">-10°F to 0°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Below -10°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20%</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Condensation on glass is a nuisance; condensation you cannot see is the real problem. The same physics plays out inside exterior walls and in the attic, where warm moist air leaks through gaps around outlets, light fixtures and the attic hatch and meets cold sheathing. Over a long winter that can wet insulation and framing without any visible sign until paint peels or a stain appears. Visible window condensation is the early warning that the house is at risk of the hidden kind, which is why it is worth taking seriously.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical approach is simple. Set a target from the table, check the windows on the first very cold mornings, and if you see more than a thin line of fog at the bottom edge of the glass, drop the setting five points. If the windows stay dry and the air still feels harsh, nudge it up. If you heat with a furnace and want this handled automatically, a <a href="/whole-house-humidifier/">whole-house humidifier</a> with an outdoor sensor does exactly this adjustment for you.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Water on the glass means you are too high for today.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Whatever the humidistat says, persistent condensation on the inside of windows is the house telling you the indoor humidity is above what your coldest surfaces can handle. Lower the setting until the glass stays clear by mid-morning.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="too-dry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Signs Your Air Is Too Dry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dry air announces itself in small, irritating ways long before a hygrometer confirms it. Static shocks from door handles and light switches are the classic sign; static builds far more easily when the air holds little moisture, and it tends to fade once humidity climbs back into the 30s. Hair that will not lie flat, clothes that cling in the dryer and blankets that crackle at night all point the same way.
          </p>

          <p>
            People notice it in their skin, lips and nose. Many find that dry indoor air leaves skin tight and itchy, lips chapped, and nasal passages uncomfortable, especially first thing in the morning after a night breathing heated air. A humidifier is not a treatment for any medical condition, and anyone with persistent symptoms should talk to a doctor, but bringing a very dry bedroom back into the comfortable band is a common and reasonable comfort measure.
          </p>

          <p>
            The house shows it too. Wood shrinks as it loses moisture, so gaps open between hardwood floor boards in winter and close again in summer. Doors and drawers loosen, joints in wood furniture can open, and stringed instruments go out of tune or, in severe cases, crack. Houseplants that like humid conditions brown at the tips. These changes happen slowly, and the seasonal floor gap in particular is normal within limits, but large or worsening movement is a sign the air is staying very dry for long periods.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you see several of these at once, measure before you buy anything. A reading in the teens or low 20s on a winter day confirms the problem. A reading in the mid 30s means the air is within the band and something else is going on, and adding moisture risks pushing the house into condensation territory for little benefit.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Too dry (under 30%)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Too humid (over 50 to 60%)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Frequent static shocks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Condensation on windows and cold pipes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dry skin, lips and nasal passages</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Musty smell, especially in closets and basements</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gaps between floorboards, loose joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mold spots on window frames, caulk and grout</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Instruments drifting out of tune</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peeling paint or bubbling wallpaper</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plants browning at the tips</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sticky, clammy feel and more dust mite activity</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="too-humid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Signs Your Air Is Too Humid
          </h2>

          <p>
            Too much humidity is the more damaging mistake, and it is easy to make with a humidifier running flat out in a small, closed room. The first sign is almost always condensation: fog or droplets on windows, water pooling on sills, beads on cold-water pipes and toilet tanks. In winter that is the window physics described above; in summer it is often warm, damp air meeting air-conditioned surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            A persistent musty smell is the next stage. It usually means something is growing, often in places that stay cool and still, such as closets on exterior walls, behind furniture pushed against outside walls, or in a basement. Black or gray spots on window frames, caulk lines and bathroom grout tell the same story. Mold generally needs sustained dampness, and keeping relative humidity below 60 percent is one of the main reasons the EPA sets that ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dust mites are the less visible concern. They do not drink; they absorb moisture from the air, and they thrive at higher relative humidity. Allergy organizations commonly recommend keeping bedroom humidity at or below about 50 percent for people sensitive to them. That makes the bedroom a place where it is easy to overshoot: a humidifier bought to stop a dry throat can, if left on high with the door shut, push the room into the range mites prefer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other clues include paint that blisters or peels on exterior walls, wallpaper seams lifting, a clammy feel to bedding and upholstery, and wood that swells until doors stick. If you see these with a humidifier running, turn it down or off first and measure; if you see them without one, the moisture is coming from somewhere else, and a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifier</a>, better ventilation or a fix to the moisture source is the answer, not a lower humidifier setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Overshooting is also a maintenance problem. Wet surfaces around a humidifier, a damp carpet beneath it or a film of white dust from an ultrasonic unit all suggest it is putting out more than the room can absorb. Keeping the unit clean and running it at the right output matters as much as the number on the dial; our guide on <a href="/how-to-clean-a-humidifier/">how to clean a humidifier</a> covers the routine that keeps it from becoming its own source of mold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measuring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Measuring It Properly: Hygrometers, Placement and the Salt Test
          </h2>

          <p>
            You cannot manage a number you are guessing at, and the built-in readout on most portable humidifiers is a poor guide. Its sensor sits a few inches from the mist outlet, in the wettest air in the room, so it often reads high or low compared with the rest of the space, and many units show only a rough figure. A separate digital hygrometer costs little and is the single most useful tool for getting humidity right. Our <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer guide</a> compares the ones worth buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even good consumer hygrometers are typically rated to within about 2 to 5 percentage points, and cheap ones can drift further, so treat the reading as a band rather than a precise number. That is fine for this job. The difference between 38 and 41 percent does not matter; the difference between 22 and 42 percent, or between 45 and 65, absolutely does. What you want is a meter you trust to tell you which band you are in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement changes the reading. Keep the hygrometer at roughly breathing or sitting height, away from the humidifier itself, out of direct sun, and not on an exterior wall or near a heating register, a bathroom door or the kitchen stove. A spot in the middle of the room, several feet from the humidifier, gives the most representative figure. If you want to know what the house as a whole is doing, put one in a central hallway rather than a bedroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            To check accuracy, use the salt test. Put a few tablespoons of table salt in a small cup, add just enough water to make a damp paste with no standing liquid, and seal the cup and the hygrometer together in a zip-top bag. Leave it at a steady room temperature for 8 to 12 hours. A saturated salt solution holds the air in the bag close to 75 percent relative humidity, so a meter reading 70 is reading about five points low, and you can mentally correct for that from then on, or recalibrate if the meter allows it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Numbers to Know</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30-50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EPA's ideal indoor range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">60%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Upper limit to stay below</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~75%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Salt test reference point</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">±2-5%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical hygrometer accuracy</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="by-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Room by Room: Bedroom, Nursery, Basement and Wood
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bedrooms are where most people first notice dry air and where most portable humidifiers end up. For sleeping, the same 30 to 50 percent band applies, and many people find the low to mid 40s comfortable in winter without risk. Because bedrooms are smaller and often closed at night, a humidifier can push them past 50 percent quickly, so a model with a built-in humidistat that cycles off at a set level is worth having. Our picks for the <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">best humidifier for a bedroom</a> favor quiet units that hold a setpoint rather than run flat out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nurseries deserve the same band with extra caution. Pediatric guidance generally favors cool-mist humidifiers over warm-mist or steam units around young children because of the burn risk from hot water, and recommends cleaning them often. Keep the unit out of reach, a few feet from the crib, and do not let the room run damp; a nursery sitting at 60 percent with condensation on the window is worse than one at 35.
          </p>

          <p>
            Basements are the exception that runs the other way. They are cool, partly below grade and usually too humid rather than too dry, even in winter, so they almost never need a humidifier. The target there is typically at or below 50 percent, controlled with a dehumidifier. We cover the basement setpoint in detail in <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a>, so we will not repeat it here beyond one point: if the main floors are dry and the basement is damp, the fix for each is different, and one machine will not do both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rooms with solid wood floors, fine furniture, pianos or stringed instruments are where steady humidity pays off most. Hardwood flooring manufacturers commonly specify an indoor range around 35 to 55 percent, and instrument makers often recommend something in the 40 to 50 percent range. What hurts wood is not one particular number so much as large swings, so for these rooms aim to keep humidity steady in the low to mid 40s through the heating season rather than letting it drop into the teens and spike back up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Large open-plan spaces and whole floors are where a single small humidifier struggles. If you are trying to raise humidity across 1,500 square feet with a unit sized for a bedroom, it will run constantly and barely move the number. Our guide on <a href="/what-size-humidifier-do-i-need/">what size humidifier you need</a> covers the gallons-per-day math, and beyond a certain size a furnace-mounted unit becomes the more practical answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="adjusting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Raise or Lower Your Indoor Humidity
          </h2>

          <p>
            To raise humidity in a single room, a portable humidifier sized to the space is the straightforward fix. Pick one with a built-in humidistat and set it to the band rather than running it on high, and confirm the result with a separate hygrometer. Evaporative models self-limit because they cannot easily push the room past the point where evaporation slows, while ultrasonic and steam units will keep adding moisture as long as they run, so the humidistat matters more with those. Our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier picks</a> cover room, large-room and whole-house options.
          </p>

          <p>
            To raise it across the whole house, a <a href="/whole-house-humidifier/">whole-house humidifier</a> installed on a forced-air furnace is the most even and lowest-maintenance approach, and the best controls adjust automatically for outdoor temperature. Small changes help too: air-drying laundry indoors, leaving the bathroom door open after a shower and keeping lids off simmering pots all add moisture, and sealing air leaks reduces how much cold, dry outside air gets drawn in to replace warm inside air.
          </p>

          <p>
            To lower humidity, start with the sources. Run bathroom fans during showers and for 20 minutes after, use the range hood when cooking, vent clothes dryers outdoors, and check that gutters and grading move water away from the foundation. If the humidifier is running, turn it down. In summer, air conditioning removes moisture as it cools; if rooms stay clammy anyway, a dehumidifier is the right tool, and in basements it is usually essential.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you do, make changes in steps and give each one a day before judging it. Humidity responds slowly in a whole house, readings swing with temperature through the day, and the windows on the coldest morning of the week are a better guide than any single reading on a mild afternoon. Aim for steady numbers inside the band, adjust with the weather, and the house and the people in it will both be more comfortable.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK SETUP</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Start at 40 percent, check the windows, adjust in five-point steps.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Set the humidifier to 40 percent, put a hygrometer mid-room, and look at the windows on the next cold morning. Fog beyond a thin line at the bottom of the glass means lower it five points; dry glass and still-harsh air means raise it five.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting one winter number and never changing it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A setting that suits a 35-degree day can soak the windows when it drops below zero. Cold glass condenses at lower humidity, so the target needs to fall as outdoor temperature falls. Check the windows on the first very cold mornings and drop the setting five points if fog spreads beyond the bottom edge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the humidifier's own readout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The sensor on most portable humidifiers sits close to the mist and reads the wettest air in the room. Use a separate hygrometer placed several feet away at sitting height, check it with the salt test once, and treat the result as the real number rather than the figure on the unit's display.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a humidifier on high in a closed bedroom</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small, closed rooms climb past 50 percent quickly, which favors dust mites and can wet the window and wall behind the unit. Use a model with a humidistat set to the low to mid 40s, or run it on low, and check the window and the floor around it in the morning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Humidifying a damp basement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Basements are usually too humid even in winter, because they are cool and partly below ground. Adding moisture there invites mustiness and mold. Measure first; most basements need a dehumidifier set around 50 percent, and the dry main floors upstairs need a separate solution.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chasing a precise number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Consumer hygrometers are typically accurate to a few points, and readings swing with temperature through the day. Getting from 22 to 40 percent matters; arguing over 41 versus 43 does not. Aim for a band, make changes in steps, and let the windows tell you when you have gone too far.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the ideal indoor humidity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The EPA recommends keeping indoor relative humidity below 60 percent and ideally between 30 and 50 percent. In practice that means 40 to 50 percent in summer, controlled with air conditioning or a dehumidifier, and 30 to 45 percent in winter, with the lower end used on the coldest days to keep windows and walls from condensing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should my house be in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start around 40 percent and adjust for outdoor temperature. Common guidance is about 40 percent when it is 20 to 40 degrees outside, 35 percent at 10 to 20 degrees, 30 percent near zero and 25 percent or lower below zero. If water collects on the inside of your windows, lower the setting until the glass stays clear.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best humidity for sleeping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most people sleep comfortably in the 40 to 50 percent range, and keeping a bedroom at or below about 50 percent is commonly recommended for people sensitive to dust mites. In winter the low to mid 40s is a sensible bedroom target. Use a humidifier with a humidistat so a closed room does not climb past it overnight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 30 percent humidity too low?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Thirty percent is at the bottom of the EPA's ideal range, so it is acceptable, and on very cold days it may be the highest your windows can handle without condensation. Many people find it slightly dry for comfort. If the windows stay clear at 30 percent, you can usually raise it toward 35 to 40 percent without problems.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 60 percent humidity too high indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, 60 percent is the upper limit the EPA recommends staying below. At that level and above, dust mites and mold grow more readily, and in winter you will almost certainly see condensation on windows. If your home sits at 60 percent or higher, turn off any humidifier and look at ventilation or a dehumidifier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I check if my hygrometer is accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the salt test. Mix a few tablespoons of table salt with just enough water to form a damp paste, seal it in a zip-top bag with the hygrometer, and leave it 8 to 12 hours at a steady temperature. The air in the bag settles near 75 percent, so the difference between that and your reading is the meter's error.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I set my humidifier to?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Set it to the low to mid 40s for most rooms in mild winter weather, and lower toward 30 to 35 percent when outdoor temperatures drop near or below zero. Avoid running it on continuous high. Confirm with a separate hygrometer, because the humidifier's built-in sensor often reads the moist air right beside the outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

