        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The <strong class="text-white">ideal indoor humidity</strong> is a band, not a number: 30 to 50 percent, never above 60, with winter settings lowered as the weather outside gets colder. Your windows are the best gauge you own, and condensation on the glass means you are over the right level for that day.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy a separate hygrometer, check it once with the salt test, and make changes in five-point steps. Humidify the rooms that are dry, dehumidify the basement that is damp, and keep whatever machine you use clean. That covers almost everything that goes wrong with indoor humidity.
          </p>
        </div>

